        customers: ["**Bajaj Auto** (the largest, and a medium-term dependence), **Honda** and **Hero**: the top three give 55–60% of revenue (CRISIL).", "**New two-wheeler customers**: ₹220cr a year from a large Japanese maker and about ₹200cr a year from a fast-growing maker, mostly starting in Q4 FY27.", "**Tata Motors, a Japanese car maker, truck makers, a US solar-tracker maker** (₹150cr+ a year) and **aircraft-engine makers** through Chester Hall."],
        difficulty: { level: 'Medium', text: "Pressing, welding and plating two-wheeler frames and exhausts at scale needs heavy automation (890+ robots) but is widely done. High-tensile steel parts, steering columns and aerospace machining are harder: aerospace approvals take 12–24 months." },
        moat: "Scale and automation in two-wheeler metal parts, a fast rescue of a Japanese maker's supply that won a ₹220cr order, and licensed high-tensile steel know-how make it a strong supplier to its three big customers. The weaknesses: a return on capital of only about 14%, customer concentration and a web of promoter-company dealings.",
        salesDrivers: ["**Two-wheeler volumes at Bajaj, Honda and Hero**: two- and three-wheelers grew 18% in Q1 against industry output of 20–25%, because the fastest-growing makers were ones it served less.", "**New orders from Q4 FY27**: Japanese and Indian two-wheeler makers, the US solar-tracker maker and an Indian electric truck maker.", "**Car and truck growth**: management wanted to double this business from FY25 levels, needing 40–45% growth this year; Q1 delivered 18%.", "**Acquisitions and capex**: ₹1,700cr raised at ₹220 in July, mostly for aerospace and car and truck deals (only Hyva named so far), plus capex of 6–6.5% of manufacturing revenue for new plants at Bangalore and Bhiwadi.", "**Merging promoter companies**: Badve Autocomps and Eximius Infra Tech are being merged in at close to book value, which lifts content per vehicle and should cut related-party flows."],
        inputs: "**Steel sheet, tube and stainless steel** for frames, exhausts and tanks, **plastics** for polymer parts, and steel bought and sold in the trading arm. Materials were 81% of sales in FY26, inflated by the trading business.",
        costEffect: "**Passed on, with a lag.** Steel prices are recovered from vehicle makers after a delay, and management says ‘the worst is behind us’ on costs, with pass-through to lift margins in coming quarters. **Rule of thumb:** a 10% rise in material costs not passed on would cut the margin by about 8 points (12% to 4%), though traded steel is bought and sold at matching prices. **What happened:** the Screener margin was 12% in FY24, FY25 and FY26, then 11.5% in Q1 FY27 against 12.4% a year earlier; manufacturing alone fell from 13.8% to 12.7% as steel costs ran ahead of prices and four new plants added staff before revenue.",
        competitors: [{ name: 'Sandhar Technologies', note: 'Rival in two-wheeler sheet-metal frames and parts, strongest at Hero and TVS.' }, { name: 'Munjal Auto and JBM Auto', note: 'Other Indian makers of two-wheeler exhausts, frames and sheet-metal assemblies.' }, { name: 'In-house and Japanese-affiliated suppliers', note: 'Vehicle makers’ own plants and affiliated suppliers compete for frames and exhausts.' }],
        position: "**One of India's top three makers of two-wheeler metal parts, with about 24% share** (prospectus data, March 2024).",
        money: "**₹567cr of net debt** on Screener at March (the company says ₹597.7cr), before the ₹1,700cr share sale in July, which should leave it with net cash until spent. CRISIL rates it AA-. FY26 operating cash was ₹876cr and capex about ₹618cr. It guarantees a promoter company's ₹41.3cr loan, has pledged ₹32.0cr of land for another promoter company's ₹214cr loan, and has US$98.75M of guarantees for the trading arm. Promoters hold **61.14%**, down from 73.01% in June 2025 after a promoter company's sale at ₹153.7 and the share issue.",
        watch: ["Related-party flows shrinking after the merger.", "Guarantees for promoter companies released.", "How the ₹1,650cr of unspent QIP money is used."],
